WEBVTT

00:00.824 --> 00:02.240
We're now going
to think about one

00:02.240 --> 00:05.230
of my most favorite
theorems in mathematics,

00:05.230 --> 00:07.180
and that's the squeeze theorem.

00:07.180 --> 00:10.460
And one of the reasons that it's
one of my most favorite theorem

00:10.460 --> 00:13.865
in mathematics is that it
has the word "squeeze" in it,

00:13.865 --> 00:15.620
a word that you
don't see showing up

00:15.620 --> 00:17.170
in a lot of mathematics.

00:17.170 --> 00:18.820
But it is appropriately named.

00:18.820 --> 00:22.400
And this is oftentimes also
called the sandwich theorem,

00:22.400 --> 00:26.190
which is also an appropriate
name, as we'll see in a second.

00:26.190 --> 00:29.320
And since it can be called
the sandwich theorem,

00:29.320 --> 00:31.730
let's first just
think about an analogy

00:31.730 --> 00:35.340
to get the intuition behind
the squeeze or the sandwich

00:35.340 --> 00:36.390
theorem.

00:36.390 --> 00:38.830
Let's say that there
are three people.

00:38.830 --> 00:46.420
Let's say that there is
Imran, let's say there's Diya,

00:46.420 --> 00:49.160
and let's say there is Sal.

00:49.160 --> 00:52.370
And let's say that
Imran, on any given day,

00:52.370 --> 00:55.880
he always has the fewest
amount of calories.

00:55.880 --> 00:57.300
And Sal, on any
given day, always

00:57.300 --> 00:59.100
has the most number of calories.

00:59.100 --> 01:02.580
So in a given day,
we can always say

01:02.580 --> 01:15.190
Diya eats at least
as much as Imran.

01:15.190 --> 01:22.370
And then we can say Sal eats
at least as much-- that's

01:22.370 --> 01:26.070
just to repeat those
words-- as Diya.

01:26.070 --> 01:28.400
And so we could set up a
little inequality here.

01:28.400 --> 01:35.610
On a given day, we could
write that Imran's calories

01:35.610 --> 01:39.880
on a given day are going to be
less than or equal to Diya's

01:39.880 --> 01:47.390
calories on that same day,
which is going to be less than

01:47.390 --> 01:50.650
or equal to Sal's
calories on that same day.

01:55.340 --> 01:57.900
Now let's say that it's Tuesday.

01:57.900 --> 02:03.090
Let's say on
Tuesday you find out

02:03.090 --> 02:11.370
that Imran ate 1,500 calories.

02:11.370 --> 02:20.460
And on that same day, Sal
also ate 1,500 calories.

02:20.460 --> 02:22.860
So based on this,
how many calories

02:22.860 --> 02:25.650
must Diya have eaten that day?

02:25.650 --> 02:28.730
Well, she always eats at least
as many as Imran's, so she

02:28.730 --> 02:31.530
ate 1,500 calories or more.

02:31.530 --> 02:35.250
But she always has less than or
equal to the number of calories

02:35.250 --> 02:36.330
Sal eats.

02:36.330 --> 02:38.094
So it must be less
than or equal to 1,500.

02:38.094 --> 02:39.510
Well, there's only
one number that

02:39.510 --> 02:41.930
is greater than or equal
to 1,500 and less than

02:41.930 --> 02:45.960
or equal to 1,500, and
that is 1,500 calories.

02:45.960 --> 02:48.570
So Diya must have
eaten 1,500 calories.

02:48.570 --> 02:50.530
This is common sense.

02:50.530 --> 02:54.890
Diya must have had
1,500 calories.

02:54.890 --> 02:56.710
And the squeeze
theorem is essentially

02:56.710 --> 02:59.740
the mathematical version
of this for functions.

02:59.740 --> 03:04.170
And you could even view this is
Imran's calories as a function

03:04.170 --> 03:07.800
of the day, Sal's calories
as a function of the day,

03:07.800 --> 03:09.840
and Diya's calories as
a function of the day

03:09.840 --> 03:12.410
is always going to
be in between those.

03:12.410 --> 03:16.950
So now let's make this a
little bit more mathematical.

03:16.950 --> 03:21.440
So let me clear this out
so we can have some space

03:21.440 --> 03:23.430
to do some math in.

03:23.430 --> 03:27.250
So let's say that we
have the same analogy.

03:27.250 --> 03:29.620
So let's say that we
have three functions.

03:29.620 --> 03:32.650
Let's say f of x
over some interval

03:32.650 --> 03:35.900
is always less
than or equal to g

03:35.900 --> 03:40.340
of x over that same interval,
which is always less than

03:40.340 --> 03:45.530
or equal to h of x over
that same interval.

03:45.530 --> 03:47.305
So let me depict
this graphically.

03:52.260 --> 03:56.030
So that is my y-axis.

03:56.030 --> 03:57.770
This is my x-axis.

04:01.810 --> 04:03.940
And I'll just
depict some interval

04:03.940 --> 04:06.080
in the x-axis right over here.

04:06.080 --> 04:12.810
So let's say h of x looks
something like that.

04:12.810 --> 04:16.190
Let me make it more interesting.

04:16.190 --> 04:17.360
This is the x-axis.

04:17.360 --> 04:22.560
So let's say h of x looks
something like this.

04:22.560 --> 04:25.290
So that's my h of x.

04:25.290 --> 04:28.940
Let's say f of x looks
something like this.

04:28.940 --> 04:32.180
Maybe it does some interesting
things, and then it comes in,

04:32.180 --> 04:35.260
and then it goes up
like this, so f of x

04:35.260 --> 04:38.050
looks something like that.

04:38.050 --> 04:39.960
And then g of x,
for any x-value, g

04:39.960 --> 04:43.297
of x is always in
between these two.

04:43.297 --> 04:45.380
And I think you see where
the squeeze is happening

04:45.380 --> 04:48.780
and where the
sandwich is happening.

04:48.780 --> 04:52.300
If h of x and f of x were
bendy pieces of bread, g of x

04:52.300 --> 04:54.060
would be the meat of the bread.

04:54.060 --> 04:56.660
So it would look
something like this.

04:56.660 --> 05:00.370
Now, let's say that we know--
this is the analogous thing.

05:00.370 --> 05:03.290
On a particular day, Sal and
Imran ate the same amount.

05:03.290 --> 05:05.240
Let's say for a
particular x-value,

05:05.240 --> 05:09.990
the limit as f and h
approach that x-value, they

05:09.990 --> 05:12.000
approach is the same limit.

05:12.000 --> 05:14.310
So let's take this
x-value right over here.

05:14.310 --> 05:18.790
Let's say the x-value
is c right over there.

05:18.790 --> 05:23.070
And let's say that
the limit of f

05:23.070 --> 05:34.960
of x as x approaches c is
equal to L. And let's say

05:34.960 --> 05:45.105
that the limit as x approaches
c of h of x is also equal to L.

05:45.105 --> 05:48.010
So notice, as x
approaches c, h of x

05:48.010 --> 05:52.100
approaches L. As x approaches
c from either side, f of x

05:52.100 --> 05:53.560
approaches L.

05:53.560 --> 05:55.484
So these limits
have to be defined.

05:55.484 --> 05:56.900
Actually, the
functions don't have

05:56.900 --> 05:58.610
to be defined at x approaches c.

05:58.610 --> 06:00.240
Just over this
interval, they have

06:00.240 --> 06:02.120
to be defined as we approach it.

06:02.120 --> 06:04.530
But over this interval,
this has to be true.

06:04.530 --> 06:06.805
And if these limits right
over here are defined

06:06.805 --> 06:09.350
and because we know that g
of x is always sandwiched in

06:09.350 --> 06:11.830
between these two
functions, therefore,

06:11.830 --> 06:14.530
on that day or
for that x-value--

06:14.530 --> 06:16.800
I should get out of that
food-eating analogy--

06:16.800 --> 06:21.910
this tells us if all of this
is true over this interval,

06:21.910 --> 06:31.560
this tells us that the limit
as x approaches c of g of x

06:31.560 --> 06:34.050
must also be equal to L.

06:34.050 --> 06:36.280
And once again, this
is common sense.

06:36.280 --> 06:39.750
f of x is approaching
L, h of x is approaching

06:39.750 --> 06:42.130
L, g of x is sandwiched
in between it.

06:42.130 --> 06:47.444
So it also has to
be approaching L.

06:47.444 --> 06:49.360
And you might say, well,
this is common sense.

06:49.360 --> 06:50.880
Why is this useful?

06:50.880 --> 06:53.640
Well, as you'll see, this
is useful for finding

06:53.640 --> 06:55.830
the limits of some
wacky functions.

06:55.830 --> 06:58.350
If you can find a function
that's always greater than it

06:58.350 --> 07:00.145
and a function that's
always less than it,

07:00.145 --> 07:02.270
and you can find the limit
as they approach some c,

07:02.270 --> 07:04.650
and it's the same
limit, then you

07:04.650 --> 07:06.490
know that that wacky
function in between

07:06.490 --> 07:09.997
is going to approach
that same limit.
